        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: When was Dunville incorporated as a town?\n\nAnswer A: 1900\n  about: Dunnville\n  sense: June 11, 1963 (the document does not state it)\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 2\n  evidence: \"Dunnville was incorporated as a village in 1860 and then as a town in 1900.\" [document 1]; \"Dunnville was incorporated as a village in 1860 and then as a town in 1900.\" [document 2]\n\nAnswer B: June 11, 1963\n  about: Dunnville\n  sense: June 11, 1963\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Dunville was incorporated as a town on June 11, 1963.\" [document 3]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: years ago. There is an impassable dam at Dunnville which regulates the level of the Grand River at Port Maitland which, in the 19th century, also helped regulate the level of the Welland Canal (from 1829 until 1887 when the third canal began to intake its water directly from Lake Erie). Dunnville was incorporated as a village in 1860 and then as a town in 1900. In 1974, the town amalgamated with the townships of Dunn, Canborough, Moulton and Sherbrooke when the Regional Municipality of Haldimand-Norfolk was formed. In 2001, Dunnville and all other municipalities within the region were dissolved\nDocument 2: the Welland `` feeder '' canal . The town was once home to several water-powered mills and a bustling canal port . However , the feeder canal closed in the late 1880s , and the last mill was replaced with a condominium complex about a decade ago . An impassable dam at Dunnville regulates the level of the Grand River at Port Maitland . In the 19th century , this dam also helped regulate the level of the Welland Canal . Dunnville was incorporated as a village in 1860 and then as a town in 1900 . In 1974 ,\nDocument 3: Dunville, Newfoundland and Labrador Dunville was earlier called Northeast or North East Placentia. The name was formed from \"Dunphy's Village\" a part of Northeast Placentia. It is a community that makes up the north-eastern section of the Town of Placentia. It stretches approximately 8km along the northern shore of the North-East Arm of Placentia Bay. The Post Office was established in 1895 and the first Post Master was Joachim Connors. Dunville was incorporated as a town on June 11, 1963. By 1966, it had a population of 1,606. Along with Freshwater, Argentia, and Jerseyside, it became part of Placentia in\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
